---
type: statement
kind: remark
---

- Note that the multiple trait methods _basically_ serve the same interface, in so far that they take mutable reference to the underlying data and use this to produce bytes.
- Note that the methods of `Rng` take a mutable reference to the underlying structure.
  The mutable nature of the reference hints at the impurities which allow for randomness.
  In other words, because each of the methods mutate the struct, it is reasonable to expect that consecutive calls do not return the same output.
- The name `Rng` is an acronym for _random number generator_ as opposed to _pseudorandom number generator_, despite the fact that there are no guarantees that the implementation actually produces random data (versus it seemingly doing so, i.e. _pseudo_).
